<header class="page-header wrap-narrow">
  <h1 class="page-title">About The AI Legal Review</h1>
</header>

<div class="prose wrap-narrow">
  <p class="lede">The AI Legal Review is a publication of the University of Nottingham AI in Law Society. It provides a platform for students and contributors to examine how artificial intelligence is changing law, legal practice, regulation and access to justice.</p>

  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>The Review aims to publish thoughtful, evidence-led work rather than generic commentary on AI. We look for writing that engages with the law as it stands, uses authority carefully, and reaches a clear argument about how artificial intelligence affects legal rights, institutions and the people who rely on them.</p>
  <p>Pieces range from analytical essays to shorter commentary on developments in regulation, the courts and legal practice.</p>

  <h2>Who we are</h2>
  <p>The Review is run by the University of Nottingham AI in Law Society, a student society. It is not an official publication of the University of Nottingham and is not a peer-reviewed academic journal. Views expressed by individual contributors are their own.</p>

  <h2>The Society</h2>
  <p>The AI Legal Review is published by the University of Nottingham AI in Law Society. For the Society’s news, events and announcements, follow it on Instagram.</p>
